     <widget class="QWidget" name="tab_credits">
      <attribute name="title">
       <string>Credits</string>
      </attribute>
      <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="tabCreditsLayout">
       <item>
        <widget class="QTextBrowser" name="text_credits">
         <property name="openExternalLinks">
          <bool>true</bool>
         </property>
         <property name="html">
          <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;body style=&quot;font-family:sans-serif;&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 style=&quot;color:#2c5f8a;&quot;&gt;Q-LandslideDam&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A QGIS plugin for modeling landslide dams and river channel obstructions using wedge geometries, with automatic calculation of the upstream dammed lake.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Author&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Pierluigi De Rosa&lt;/b&gt; (University of Perugia)&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;a href=&quot;mailto:pierluigi.derosa@gmail.com&quot;&gt;pierluigi.derosa@gmail.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Funding and acknowledgements&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Q-LandslideDam plugin was developed by Pierluigi De Rosa (University of Perugia) as part of the project &amp;ldquo;LANDAM &amp;ndash; LANdslide DAMs: Characterization, Prediction, and Emergency Management.&amp;rdquo; This project is included in the activities of the RETURN (multi-Risk sciEnce for resilienT commUnities undeR a changiNg climate) - Spoke 2 &amp;ldquo;Ground Instabilities&amp;rdquo;, funded by the European Union &amp;ndash; NextGenerationEU - PNRR, Mission 4, Component 2, Investment 1.3; D.D. 1243 of August 2, 2022, PE0000005.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Project&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Repository: &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/pierluigiderosa/Q-LandslideDam&quot;&gt;github.com/pierluigiderosa/Q-LandslideDam&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;
Bug reports: &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/pierluigiderosa/Q-LandslideDam/issues&quot;&gt;issue tracker&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Built with&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://qgis.org&quot;&gt;QGIS&lt;/a&gt; and PyQGIS&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://grass.osgeo.org/grass-stable/manuals/r.lake.html&quot;&gt;GRASS GIS r.lake&lt;/a&gt; for filling the dammed lake&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;NumPy and GDAL&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;License&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This program is free software, released under the terms of the GNU General Public License version 2 or later.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
         </property>
        </widget>
       </item>
      </layout>
     </widget>
     <widget class="QWidget" name="tab_instructions">
      <attribute name="title">
       <string>Instructions</string>
      </attribute>
      <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="tabInstructionsLayout">
       <item>
        <widget class="QTextBrowser" name="text_instructions">
         <property name="openExternalLinks">
          <bool>true</bool>
         </property>
         <property name="html">
          <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;body style=&quot;font-family:sans-serif;&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 style=&quot;color:#2c5f8a;&quot;&gt;Instructions&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;DEM raster&lt;/b&gt;: select the DEM. It must use a projected CRS in meters (e.g. UTM); geographic coordinates and Pseudo-Mercator (EPSG:3857) are not allowed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Landslide point&lt;/b&gt;: press &lt;i&gt;Capture Coordinates&lt;/i&gt; and click on the map where the landslide reaches the river channel, or type the X and Y coordinates manually.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Landslide parameters&lt;/b&gt;:
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Landslide direction&lt;/i&gt;: direction of landslide movement, in degrees from North, clockwise (0–359°); the dam crest axis lies along it; see &lt;i&gt;Landslide direction&lt;/i&gt; below;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Face slope (upstream/downstream)&lt;/i&gt;: slope of the upstream and downstream faces, same value for both (typically 25–40°);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Crest slope (along axis)&lt;/i&gt;: slope of the crest along its axis, from the selected point towards both valley sides; 0° = horizontal crest (typically 0–10°);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Volume&lt;/i&gt;: volume of the landslide deposit (m³).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Settings&lt;/b&gt;: advanced parameters of the iterative algorithm (vertical step, maximum iterations, maximum radius).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Output&lt;/b&gt;: check &lt;i&gt;Save raster permanently&lt;/i&gt; to choose where to save the modified DEM; otherwise it is saved to a temporary folder. &lt;i&gt;Load detailed results table and lake control points&lt;/i&gt; (checked by default) adds the run results table and the lake upstream/downstream control points to the Layers panel as temporary layers; uncheck it to skip them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Press &lt;b&gt;OK&lt;/b&gt; to run the calculation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Dam geometry&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The dam is a four-sided pyramid with its apex at the selected point. Its surface is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;z = (z&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; + h) − |d&lt;sub&gt;axis&lt;/sub&gt;| · tan(crest slope) − |d&lt;sub&gt;river&lt;/sub&gt;| · tan(face slope)&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where &lt;i&gt;z&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/i&gt; is the DEM elevation at the selected point, &lt;i&gt;h&lt;/i&gt; is the dam height (raised step by step until the target volume is reached), &lt;i&gt;d&lt;sub&gt;axis&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/i&gt; is the distance along the crest axis and &lt;i&gt;d&lt;sub&gt;river&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/i&gt; the distance across it (upstream–downstream). The body is clipped by the terrain (valley sides) and by the &lt;i&gt;Max Radius&lt;/i&gt; set in Settings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;
  SECTION ALONG THE RIVER        SECTION ALONG THE CREST
  (upstream → downstream)        (bank → bank)

            crest                        crest slope
             /\                          ___●___
   face     /  \     face           ____/       \____
  slope    /    \    slope
      ____/      \____
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The model is symmetric: upstream and downstream faces have the same slope, and along the landslide direction the crest descends equally in both senses. As a consequence, directions θ and θ+180° (e.g. 310° and 130°) produce the same deposit.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Landslide direction&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Enter the direction in which the landslide &lt;b&gt;moves&lt;/b&gt;, measured &lt;b&gt;clockwise from North&lt;/b&gt; (0° = towards North, 90° = towards East, 180° = towards South, 270° = towards West). The dam crest axis is laid along this direction, so the landslide crosses the valley from the source slope to the opposite bank; the upstream and downstream faces are perpendicular to it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;
            N (0°)
            ↑
   W (270°) ●  E (90°)       ● = selected point
            S (180°)

  Example: river flowing West → East,
           landslide from the southern slope moving North (0°)

        northern valley side
     ~~~~~~~~|~~~~~~~~
  →  river → ● → river →     crest axis = landslide direction
     ~~~~~~~~↑~~~~~~~~
        southern slope (source)
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;table border=&quot;1&quot; cellpadding=&quot;4&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot;&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;River flows&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Landslide source slope&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Landslide direction&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;East–West&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;southern&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;0°&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;East–West&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;northern&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;180°&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;North–South&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;western&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;90°&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;North–South&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;eastern&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;270°&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A landslide perpendicular to the river gives a dam straight across the valley. An oblique direction is allowed and gives an oblique dam: the closer the direction is to the river direction, the more the dam lies along the valley instead of across it, so check that it actually crosses the channel.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; North is the &lt;i&gt;grid North&lt;/i&gt; of the DEM CRS (the +Y axis of the map), not true geographic North. In UTM the difference (meridian convergence) is at most a few degrees and is usually negligible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Results&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;modified DEM including the dam, with hillshade;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;upstream dammed lake (computed with GRASS &lt;i&gt;r.lake&lt;/i&gt;) with water level, volume, area and maximum depth;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;lake control points layer;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;results table with all the details of the run (see below).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If the lake reaches the DEM edge it may be truncated: in that case use a larger DEM. When the GRASS provider is not active in Processing, the lake is computed with an internal NumPy fallback.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Results table&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each run adds a temporary table without geometry named &lt;i&gt;Q-LandslideDam results YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS&lt;/i&gt; (date and time of the run), so that results of different runs can be told apart. Open it with &lt;i&gt;Open Attribute Table&lt;/i&gt;; it is not saved to disk and can be removed at any time (use &lt;i&gt;Make Permanent&lt;/i&gt; to keep it). Each row is one value; the columns are:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;section&lt;/b&gt;: group of the value (Run, Input, Settings, Dam, Lake);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;parameter&lt;/b&gt;: name of the value;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;value&lt;/b&gt;: numeric value (empty for text entries);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;unit&lt;/b&gt;: unit of measure (m, m a.s.l., m², ha, m³, °, %);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;text&lt;/b&gt;: text value (names, file paths, status, notes).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Main parameters:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Run&lt;/b&gt;: date and time, DEM layer, CRS, cell size and path of the output dam DEM;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Input&lt;/b&gt; / &lt;b&gt;Settings&lt;/b&gt;: the values entered in the dialog and in Settings;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Dam&lt;/b&gt;:
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Iterations&lt;/i&gt;: number of height steps needed to reach the target volume;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Computed landslide volume&lt;/i&gt;: volume of the modeled deposit;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Volume difference vs target&lt;/i&gt;: (computed − target) / target, in %;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Max deposit thickness&lt;/i&gt;: maximum thickness of the deposit above the original DEM;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Dam footprint area&lt;/i&gt;: plan area covered by the deposit;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Lake&lt;/b&gt;:
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Status&lt;/i&gt;: &lt;i&gt;ok&lt;/i&gt; (lake impounded, spilling downstream), &lt;i&gt;border&lt;/i&gt; (spilling towards the DEM edge, lake probably truncated), &lt;i&gt;not_effective&lt;/i&gt; (the dam does not block the valley) or &lt;i&gt;error&lt;/i&gt;; &lt;i&gt;Note&lt;/i&gt; explains it;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Max dam crest elevation&lt;/i&gt;: highest elevation of the dam;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Spillway level (water surface)&lt;/i&gt;: elevation at which the lake starts to overflow; it is the lake water level;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Freeboard (crest − spillway)&lt;/i&gt;: height of the dam crest above the water level;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Spill outlet&lt;/i&gt;: where the water leaves the lake;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Lake volume (numpy)&lt;/i&gt; / &lt;i&gt;(r.lake)&lt;/i&gt;: stored water volume, computed internally and, if GRASS is available, from the r.lake raster;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Lake area&lt;/i&gt;: flooded area, in m² and ha;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Max water depth&lt;/i&gt;: maximum depth of the lake (water level minus lake bottom);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;Upstream seed&lt;/i&gt; / &lt;i&gt;Downstream point&lt;/i&gt;: coordinates and elevations of the control points used to fill the lake (also in the &lt;i&gt;Lake - control points&lt;/i&gt; layer).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
         </property>
        </widget>
       </item>
